**Performance targets and benchmarks**
- The public numbers come from The Benchmarker, fetched 2026-11-19. Requests per second at 64 concurrent connections:

| Framework | req/s |
|---|---|
| Vert.x | 199,842 |
| Workerman (raw) | 173,597 |
| Quarkus | 151,114 |
| Gin | 111,896 |
| webman | 110,952 |
| Spring | 58,750 |
| Laravel | 1,741 |

- **Correction during review:** the first draft said webman ran 138,903 req/s and beat Gin by 1.24×. When I re-fetched the page it showed 110,952, so that claim was wrong. All the derived ratios have been recalculated. The honest comparison: about level with Gin, about 70% of Quarkus, about 56% of Vert.x. The webman framework layer costs about 36% compared with raw Workerman.
- The benchmark site is a rolling dataset, so the skill now says to record the fetch date and re-fetch when auditing.
- Our own pass/fail bars are only estimates until 1.0 stress tests are run and are marked "须 1.0 压测定基线". They are 80,000 req/s for hello world, 5,000 req/s with the auth chain plus one database query, recovery within 5 s after a process is killed, and memory growth ≤10% over a 24-hour soak.
- Workerman's five strengths (resident memory, multi-process event loop, built-in WebSocket/SSE, custom processes, smooth reload) are each tied to a checklist item, along with the risk each one brings.
- The Go/Java comparison covers six areas: throughput and latency, connection pooling, rate limiting and circuit breaking, observability, graceful shutdown and zero-downtime release, and config management. Each says how far webman can get, why it falls short, and how to close the gap.

